**Ootl** (/'*uːtəl*/, *[[drueidian|Drueidian]]*: ⵧⴻⵍ), colloquially known as the **Rootfather**, is one of the seven elemental deities. He is bound to the element of Earth and he is the god of nature. The Otlan religion is very secluded, yet filled with liveliness and stoicism.

-----

# Religion
The Otlan religion is one which occurs secluded from other society. By no means are the *Bandrui* [^bandrui] separated from society, but their religion is. They have constructed secluded shrines around five large stone spires. Most of these spires aren't natural, yet they look identical. When a ceremony starts the *Bandrui*, the followers of Ootl, decorate the shrine with large ribbons connecting the stone spires with the outer wooden pillar circle. Apart from the *Cosantóir*[^cosantoir], who lives nearby to guard the shrine, there isn't any form of civilization in the near area.

The seasonal celebrations of the Otlan religion are the only reason to set up the shrine. These celebrations take place on the equinox's and the solstice's around midday, which they call the *Eye of the Sun*. There aren't much song and dance or big fires. They are surprisingly stoic and strict. It looks more like ceremony than a celebration, but as the ceremony comes to a closes and the communal rites were completed. Then does the fun begin, as there are: dancing lights along the ribbons, barrels of wine being opened, fire barrel's being lit and the stoicism of the ceremony is coming off. And when morning comes, the once neat shrine has been taken over by the celebration at night. Small tents are scattered throughout and the *Bandrui* are waking up to restore the shrine to it's original state.

Outside of the larger seasonal celebrations, the *Bandrui* perform a series of Rituals and Rites throughout their day to day life. The most important and mandatory of these are dusk and dawn rituals. An ritual is something very persona, as it symbolizes your connection to Ootl. An ritual can be completed to ones own interpretation. Yet there are four mandatory guidelines for an ritual. These are: acknowledgement of the three worlds: the land, the sea and the sky; the acknowledgement of the four directions, the veneration of the ancestors and ceremoniously invoking the name of Ootl.

The Otlan religion and the *Bandrui* are heavily monotheistic. In any nation where they're an official religion, they're the only one. Worship of any other deity is forbidden and heavily punished. In other states there exist smaller Otlan Clanns[^clann], which are completely operated from civilization. Because their doctrine states that any civilization other than their own is horrendous.

Ootl himself is said to be as old as time itself. The story of his ascension didn't survive the passage of time. A very old journal mentioned the supposed story appearing, but never the actual story itself.

## Sects
- Madrama of **Damhain**[^damhain] 
Damhain is the earth centered Madrama. These clann's have a higher regard for nature and life in general. The integration of plants, earth and rock into their shrines and rituals is an essential part.
- Madrama of **Cloch**[^cloch]
Cloch is the stone centered Madrama. They are found in the rock-lands and have built there shrines into the existing large stone spires. Adding to that, their homes are constructed inside these rocks as-well.
- Madrama of **Ainmhí**[^ainmhi]
Ainmhi is the Madrama of Animals. They are the more fauna friendly Clanns. Their lifestyle puts very little stress on nature. As such they feel entitled to use its resources to survive. Due to the differences in biome's, they sometimes substitute the stone spires from ones made out of earth or wood.
- Madrama of **Cossaint**[^cossaint]
Cossaint is the Madrama of Protection. These are the warrior class of the Otlan society. Their primary goal is to defend, in contrary to other warrior classes. They somehow manage to attend most of the Otlan rituals and defend its society.
- Madrama of **Truailithe**[^truailithe]
Truailithe is the Madrama of Corruption and Dread. This Madrama is shunned by the other ones. As such they live in complete secrecy outside of their Clann. They revel in pollution and decay.

## Structure
### Venerated Ancestors
*See also: [[list-of-otlan-venerated-ancestors|List of Otlan Venerated Ancestors]]*
The Venerated ancestors in the Otlan religion hold little recognition among the general public. A *Clann*[^clann] typically venerates one or multiple of their former members from the local area in this regard. They are addressed with the *Pan* prefix, which is given to them posthumously.

### Priestly Order
The Otlan religion is an individual, with irregular ceremonies. The follower are the *Bandrui*[^bandrui] and are part of a specific *Clann*[^clann]. This Clann headed by the *Archbandrui*, but under them there is a complete flat hierarchy. Some *Bandrui* per Clann take the title *Cosantóir*[^cosantoir] . They are tasked with the protection of the shrine and the sounding of the rooted alarm^[A natural alarm sending frequencies through the roots to the residences of the druids].

# Cultural
## Focail na Gcrann[^fng]
Unlike other religions, Otlan has no centralized holy book of his beliefs and religion. Yet the Focail na Gcrann still exists. It is a holy stone from the in the mythological ages, it outlines the basic Otlan beliefs. The *Bandrui*[^bandrui] later created the first Temple, using the same name, around the Focail na Gcrann, which would form the basis of the all the future shrines constructed. The Focail na Grcann was destructed far before [[sorrows-dawn|Sorrow's Dawn]] by unknown perpetrators. Yet the transcript has been preserved and now serves as the major holy texts in the Otlan relgion.

## Lia Fail[^lf]
Lia Fail is an major outdoor Otlan shrine located in. Per oral tradition, the central stone reaches thousand of miles down and reaches the middle of the planet. The shrine's importance rose with he destruction of the Focail na Gcrann[^fng] and the rise of magical influence in the area. The Tribann symbolizes the central and the four outer stones.

## Pilgrimages
A large element of the Otlan religion is a pilgrimage. The Focail na Gcrann[^fng] dictates that every *Bandrui*[^bandrui] must go on an *máirseáil*[^mairseail]. This implies a pilgrimage to another *Clann*'s shrine to partake in a Celebration. The destiniation is up tot he pilgrim, but there are multiple Clann's with a lot of prestige when it comes to an *máirseáil*. Along the way, one must build three stone ladders. These stone cairns symbolise every step of the journey. 

## Rooted Stones
Rooted Stones is one of the five Otlan celebrations in a year. It looks and feels like the other, but here are a few difference makers. Firstly, it usually is the end for one's *máirseáil*[^mairseail]. The Rituals and Rites for Rooted Stones are unique to this day. Starting at midday in the Eye of the Sun, they start with the first of three worlds, the Land. Meditating to form an connection with the earth below. The second is the sky, they light several fire with only the light from the sky. Lastly is the sea, using water from a nearby source. They bless it and clean their aura.

At nightfall, the stoicism once again comes off. Here the celebration evolves into dances, song and drinks being shared around the shrine. Large drums and loud shanty's dominate the space. When morning falls, all that's left is parting ceremony. Memento's made and exchanged, prayers said and rituals completed. The second leg of the *máirseáil* awaits^[I.e returning home form a pilgrimage].

## Drueidian[^drueidian]
*See also: [[drueidian|Drueidian]]*
Drueidian is the language which developed separately form the others in the Otlan religion. Most native Drueidian speakers also fluently speak another language. Its language shares no similarities or lineages with any languages, suggesting it was created at some point in history. Yet this vital piece of information is unknown.

